V8 SPARC64 bring-up
==================

Baseline: Node v26.12.1, bc2e01dcf6acd5cf7d169b5f26cf89abaaeb41f1 Bundled
V8: 14.6.202.34. Merge future updates from upstream/v26.x.

Sparkplug development checkout:
/home/stian/git/sparc/node-sparc64-jit
/root/node-sparc64-jit on T7

The preserved jitless checkout remains at node-sparc64 on both hosts.
Read the parent workspace's CLAUDE.md and this repository's AGENTS.md.
Keep port changes local until the user approves publication. Upstreaming
is deferred until the port is stable, passes its tests, and has working
JIT. The jitless V8 and Node application milestones below were reached
on 2026-10-26. See the Sparkplug section at the end for the development
configuration and its separate validation. The hardening checkpoint and current baseline Wasm milestone are
documented in the final sections.

Validation as of 2026-10-26
-------------------------

T7, GCC 16.2.1: 94/94 assembler and macro assembler tests passed with
--full. x64, GCC 13.3.0: 5/5 portable encoder/register tests passed. T7
log: out/sparc64-full-assembler-test.log Available disk: local 54 GB; T7
177 GB.

Native mksnapshot completes snapshot generation, and the normal d8 target
builds a 23 MB executable. d8 --jitless executes JavaScript on T7. The full
standard mjsunit runner selected 6,412 applicable cases in the jitless
variant: all succeeded, with zero unexpected failures. The runner applied
upstream build/variant exclusions plus five feature-applicability exclusions:
three denormal-flushing tests (the SPARC FPU helper has no flush-to-zero
support) and two Temporal regressions when Temporal is disabled. Runtime
JIT is disabled; eight JIT-only entrypoints retain explicit jitless trap
guards described below.

Logs: out/sparc64-d8-build.log, out/sparc64-full-assembler-test.log,
out/sparc64-mjsunit-jitless.log, out/sparc64-mjsunit-jitless.json.
The earlier diagnostic run selected 6,417 cases and found ten failures.
The five applicable failures were fixed by capturing Linux SPARC sigcontext
and its spilled V8 FP for the sampler, using unaligned JSON word reads,
and handling EINVAL from oversized virtual mappings. All five targeted
regressions passed before the complete final run.

The native tests compare 85 instruction words with GNU assembler and
execute generated code. Coverage includes integer arithmetic, ICC/XCC,
delay slots and annul, big-endian load widths, FP arguments and upper FP
registers, C stack and mixed arguments, 256-level C++ reentry, CASX, PC-
relative literals, forward/backward label fixups, buffer growth and
literal patching. All builtin interface-descriptor argument registers are
checked for duplicates and reserved-register use.

The macro assembler uses flat internal calls and l5 as the unbiased V8
frame pointer, preserving native i6. Its stack operand and Move helpers
account for the SPARC stack bias plus the 176-byte native spill/argument
area below the logical V8 stack. Common, typed and standard frames store
actual caller PCs (o7 + 8), matching V8's frame constants. The recursive
frame test now uses these helpers and the C-call bridge; C++ forces FLUSHW
and walks the frame chain at depths 0, 1, 8, 64 and 256.

The fast C-call bridge aligns the native stack, opens a register window,
passes up to 256 integer arguments through six registers and stack slots,
restores the original stack and preserves native i6. Native stack
arguments stay below the parent window's spill area. The bridge copies l7
into the native window so regexp interpreter trampolines can access V8's
root data. It records the caller FP/PC through V8's actual IsolateData
offsets and clears the FP on return. Tests check both stack alignments,
all six argument registers, two-register C++ return values, metadata
visible from C++, and exact SP restoration using synthetic root storage.
This fast-call path cannot trigger GC; runtime CEntry uses a separate
exit-frame call path described below. Actual isolate/GC integration
remains pending. The macro C-call bridge has executable stack-
argument checks, including a forced window flush and the last argument of
a 256-argument call. Mixed FP C arguments still have only low-level
encoder tests; simplified C linkage rejects FP signatures, as on PPC.
Floating-point operations include unary double arithmetic and all 16
predicates on each of four FCC registers, including NaNs and signed zero.
Register shifts check width/count masking. Unaligned integer memory tests
cover 1/2/4/8-byte big-endian accesses at all byte alignments, signed
extension, large displacements and base/value aliasing. Float32 bit moves
cover all 16 single-register pairs; float64 moves cover all 32 double
registers. Tests preserve signed zero, infinities, subnormals and signaling
and quiet NaN payloads without arithmetic conversion. FP memory tests
check positive/negative large displacements and untouched adjacent bytes.
The compiler branch helper executes in native tests across signed and
unsigned 32/64-bit comparisons with dirty upper word bits, add/sub overflow,
and float32/float64 ordered/unordered predicates. Native single-precision
arithmetic is checked against GCC, including NaNs, infinities and signed
zero. The float32 selector marks comparisons and negation as single
precision rather than inheriting PPC's internal double representation.

The compiler constructs common, typed and JS frames, allocates spills,
saves requested callee registers, initializes tagged slots, and restores
frames on return. JS returns drop the larger of the actual and formal
argument counts; stub returns also handle dynamic argument popping. Tail
calls restore the caller FP/PC and adjust SP around gap moves. Native C
entry uses SAVE/RESTORE, remaps six arguments into the compiler's outgoing
registers, and shifts incoming stack-argument accesses by the extra
176-byte native frame. Native tests call a generated C entry with eight
arguments, force window spills through a C++ callback, check two-register
returns, and verify tail-frame state and dynamic stack dropping. OSR and
native C tail calls remain unsupported. The frame emitter object compiles
through the production GYP graph; no full compiler-generated builtin has
executed yet.

The full assembler and shared assembler, relocation and register-config
sources pass native syntax checks. The assembler, base assembler, shared
assembler and relocation objects also build through the real GYP graph.
The complete v8_base_without_compiler static library builds on T7 through
the production GYP graph, including the new macro assembler, frame
accessors and unwinder source. Build log: out/sparc64-base-build.log.
Postmortem metadata remains enabled; its wasm-only Map field is guarded in
the generator so the lite build can compile. Native Torque, v8_libbase and
generated Torque/bytecode headers build. Production macro object log:
out/sparc64-macro-gyp-build.log.

Node and d8 now build and execute on SPARC64; see the application results below.
The snapshot compiler source set always selects the real
compiler, matching GN even when runtime TurboFan is disabled, and
directly depends on Abseil. Its architecture-independent sources now
build into libv8_compiler_for_mksnapshot_source_set.a on T7. The SPARC
instruction selector now compiles through the production GYP graph, with
191 selector visitor/helper definitions. It uses signed 13-bit
immediates, explicit unaligned-access markers and conservative machine-
operator flags. SIMD is not advertised. The new instruction scheduler
disables reordering during bring-up.

The SPARC code-generator source implements register/stack and constant
moves, swaps, and temporary stack slots for the gap resolver. Float32
moves use the even single lane of each logical FP register and preserve
raw bits. Heap-object constants use the shared root/constants table when
generating isolate-independent code; numeric constants use Smi values or
deferred heap-number allocation. The code-generator and scheduler
objects compile through the production GYP graph. This now implements
the first scalar instruction-dispatch pass: integer arithmetic and
shifts, integer/FP comparisons, scalar loads/stores, unaligned accesses,
float32/float64 arithmetic and bitcasts, stack argument pushes,
frame/root addresses, stack checks and switch dispatch. Atomic scalar
loads/stores use full memory barriers. Unsupported opcodes fail
explicitly during generation; some floating-point conversions, rotations,
and several call variants remain incomplete.
Frame construction and returns are implemented for the current non-OSR
build. Compiler-generated builtins execute in native d8. Logs:
out/sparc64-code-generator-build.log and out/sparc64-move-macro-
build.log.

The native mksnapshot executable now builds and links (43 MB on T7). Its
builtin initialization interfaces include strict alignment, Smi moves, root-table
indirection for isolate-independent external references and native entry/exit
helpers. The old inventory out/sparc64-missing-backend-symbols.txt predates the
selector/code-generator implementation. The current missing builtin-symbol
inventory out/sparc64-missing-builtins.txt is empty. Build log:
out/sparc64-mksnapshot-build.log.

Native entry/exit, the selected builtin compiler backend, snapshot
serialization and jitless JavaScript execution now work. Dedicated GC/ABI
stress and runtime JIT remain later milestones.
Branch fixups currently check displacement bounds; long-branch trampolines
are pending.

Commands
--------

Run from the Node checkout:

    python3 tools/sparc64/test-assembler.py

Requires a C++20 compiler, Python and Ninja. CXX selects the compiler. The
driver builds incrementally with four jobs. Arguments are forwarded to
GoogleTest, for example --gtest_filter=... . The default target runs 5
tests on x64 or 20 on native SPARC64 without a working isolate.

Prepare generated headers and libraries on native T7:

    sh tools/sparc64/configure.sh
    make -C out -j96 BUILDTYPE=Release torque v8_libbase
    make -C out -j96 BUILDTYPE=Release run_torque generate_bytecode_builtins_list
    make -C out -j96 BUILDTYPE=Release v8_base_without_compiler
    make -C out -j96 BUILDTYPE=Release v8_compiler_for_mksnapshot_source_set
    python3 tools/sparc64/check-assembler.py
    python3 tools/sparc64/test-assembler.py --full

The --full target runs 81 tests and links the real V8 assembler, flag,
allocation, macro assembler and CPU sources with v8_libbase/Abseil. It
uses V8_JITLESS, LTO and section garbage collection to avoid unrelated
engine dependencies; it does not provide replacement runtime
implementations. Its output is in out/sparc64-assembler-full. The default
output is out/sparc64-assembler.

The configure script disables runtime compiler tiers, Intl, Temporal and
unneeded Node dependencies during bring-up. Lite mode defines both
V8_LITE_MODE and V8_JITLESS, matching upstream GN's readonly jitless
build. SPARC64 regexp execution uses the existing bytecode interpreter.
The native regexp assembler is pending; its native stack-guard reference
is omitted from the SPARC64 jitless reference table rather than replaced
by a stub. The builtin compiler backend builds; its instruction emission remains incomplete. GN source
lists and Node GYP source selection include the new assembler files; a
standalone GN toolchain is pending. The test runner recognizes sparc64,
but its generated build metadata still needs integration with the Node GYP
build before mjsunit can run.

Use itx t7 -c '...' for native work. The wrapper returns success even when
the remote command fails: use set -e and check an explicit success marker.
Use T7 despite its existing load, as requested. For large native builds,
start at -j96. Check free disk before downloads/builds; /tmp shares the
root filesystem on the development host and T7.

For isolated native checks during a large build, SPARC64_SOURCE_OVERLAY
may name a directory containing replacement V8 paths (src/...,
include/..., etc.). The test driver places that include directory first
and selects replacement source files when present, sharing the main
generated headers and libraries. It writes to
out/sparc64-assembler[-full]-overlay. This avoids modifying headers used
by another live build. The last default --full run passed 94/94; an
earlier overlay run passed 43/43 before the native root-register
propagation check.
The full mksnapshot rebuild after adding compiler frames and returns
reached linking. The compiler backend objects build in the production
archive; remaining link failures included assembly builtins, deoptimizer
accessors, the cppgc register scanner, and missing Maglev graph-building
sources. GYP's common compiler source selection now includes GN's
!v8_enable_maglev source set, which Turboshaft needs even without Maglev
code generation. The generic graph builder declares SPARC64's hardware
rounding and CLZ capabilities unavailable, selecting existing fallback
paths. mksnapshot now links and the normal d8 target runs it for snapshot
creation. Code-object calls/tail calls, caller-register saves/restores, and
C calls now compile. SPARC64 uses the restrictive load/store normalization
needed by its selector; this removed a crash during builtin compilation.
The next missing emitter was ArchStoreWithWriteBarrier. The new emitter
uses the native barrier helper with dynamic slot addresses and ephemeron
key handling. Snapshot compilation then reached multiplication with a high
32-bit result and unsigned 64-bit-to-double conversion. Both are now
implemented, along with signed/unsigned 32/64-bit integer conversions to
float32/float64. Float-to-integer truncation now handles signed/unsigned
32/64-bit results, optional success outputs, and float32 inputs for try
conversions. Raw double-word extraction/construction and software CLZ32/64
are also implemented. All 94 native tests pass, including 33,400
integer-to-float comparisons against C++ casts, truncation boundaries,
multiplication register-overlap cases, and the expanded barrier checks.
NaN quieting preserves payloads and non-NaN bits. Float32/float64 width
conversions use native FSTOD/FDTOS instructions; tests cover subnormal
values, signed zero, infinities, NaNs, and register aliases. Math operations
use the native C bridge with fixed FP argument/result registers. Native
tests cover unary/binary/modulo calls, window flushing and stack alignment.
High-word multiplication and signed/unsigned division/remainder now cover
32/64-bit operands, register aliases, and overflow/zero-divisor boundaries.
Typed-array atomic RMW operations use CASX loops preserving adjacent
big-endian lanes; native checks cover every width/operation and eight-thread
contention, including unique fetch-add return values. Snapshot compilation
also uses the dynamic builtin-entry-table emitter and float32/float64
minimum/maximum with correct NaN and signed-zero handling. All currently
selected snapshot builtin instructions compile. Snapshot generation,
embedded assembly, d8 linking and JavaScript execution now succeed.
Build log: out/sparc64-d8-build.log; first full mjsunit run:
out/sparc64-mjsunit-first.log and out/sparc64-mjsunit-first.json. Final
passing run: out/sparc64-mjsunit-jitless.log/.json.

The SPARC deoptimizer source is now selected by GN/GYP and builds into the
production base archive. Saved float32 state reads the first word of each
big-endian saved double, preserving NaN bits. Native tests exercise saved
register reads, forward/backward deopt jump patches, and code zapping that
preserves embedded-object relocation slots using the real relocation
writer and iterator. Jump patching currently uses BA plus a delay-slot NOP
and checks the +/-8 MB displacement bound. Native snapshot generation now finalizes and serializes code; the relocation test constructs its stream
with RelocInfoWriter rather than finalizing a heap Code object.

The native cppgc register scanner opens a register window, executes
FLUSHW, and passes an unbiased stack marker to the callback. Its native
test clears the caller's spill area before placing distinct values in
local/input registers and verifies all sixteen saved window registers in
the callback, including native FP/PC. The scanner is selected in GN and
GYP's host/target heap-base source lists and resolves in mksnapshot.

The SPARC disassembler implements the common NameConverter and
Disassembler interfaces, including the scalar integer/FP instructions
emitted so far, native upper double-register encodings, signed memory
displacements, ICC/XCC/FCC branches, prediction/annul bits and CASX. Unknown
words print as raw data. Tests decode all 85 GNU assembler reference
words and check representative operand/condition text. Its production
object builds and resolves the disassembler linker symbols. The portable
syntax check also passes with warnings treated as errors and the same
shared-header comment suppression used by the test driver.

JavaScript entry builtins are now selected in GN and GYP and compile into
the production initializer archive: JSEntry, JSConstructEntry,
JSRunMicrotasksEntry and RunMicrotasksTrampoline. The native entry window
preserves C callee state and remaps the six incoming arguments for the
internal trampoline. Entry frames save/clear/restore C-entry and fast-call
metadata, track outermost versus nested entry, and link an unbiased handler
record. The shared exception landing pad records the exception and returns
the exception root sentinel; the epilogue works with FP cleared by unwinding.

Native tests execute the entry frame and handler helpers, preserve all five
non-root C arguments, inspect metadata from C++ after FLUSHW, mutate it to
check restoration, exercise a cleared-FP handler-unwind path, and reenter
through a nested construct entry from C++. The builtin wrappers pass native
syntax checking and production compilation. Native d8 now executes builtins and bytecode handlers through these entry
paths. The broad mjsunit run provides VM-level validation; dedicated
GC-stress coverage remains separate. CEntry is described below.

CEntry now compiles into the production initializer archive for stack and
register argv, one/two-register results, and ordinary/builtin exit frames.
It passes argc/argv/isolate to native C++, tests the exception sentinel,
restores context and drops stack arguments on normal return. Exceptions
call the existing no-GC runtime handler search and jump through its pending
context/FP/SP/entrypoint metadata. FP-only handlers retain a valid native
SP until the handler resets it, avoiding an invalid window spill area.

The exit-frame helpers publish an initialized saved-SP/PC slot before the
C-entry FP. Their native call helper records the actual C-call continuation
PC where ExitFrame::FillState expects it, aligns/open/restores a native
window, passes register/stack arguments, and preserves two-register results.
As in the PPC helper, a GC-capable call requires an immovable call site;
this applies to the embedded CEntry builtin. The separate fast-call helper
retains its no-GC contract.

Native checks cover five exit-frame types, both stack alignments, extra
stack space, exact return-PC metadata versus GCC's extracted return address,
context restoration, paired results, eight C arguments with FLUSHW, and
pending-handler jumps with context, cleared FP, or no replacement SP. These
checks use synthetic isolate fields; mjsunit adds real VM coverage.
Dedicated moving-GC/API stress remains separate.

The JavaScript entry trampolines load context, copy indirect handle arguments,
push the receiver, initialize unused JS registers to undefined, and invoke
Call or Construct. Runtime call helpers select the existing CEntry builtin.
Argument-copy native tests exercise raw values and indirect handles from zero
to 255 arguments. Stack-overflow checks use the real native SP so that the
176-byte spill/argument area stays above the limit; tests cover signed space
boundaries and a count register that overlaps the usual address scratch.

Call dispatch now checks Smi targets, callable JS function ranges, bound
functions, proxies, wrapped functions, class constructors and exotic callable
objects. Construct dispatch checks the constructor bit before choosing a
function, bound function, proxy or native-context delegate. ConstructFunction
selects the generic or builtin construct stub from SharedFunctionInfo flags.
Function.prototype.call/apply and Reflect.apply/construct adapt stack arguments
and preserve upstream missing-argument and error-message behavior. These
builtins pass native syntax checking and production compilation/link-symbol
resolution. Native d8 and mjsunit now exercise generated code in real isolates.

Function invocation now adapts missing arguments by moving the receiver and
existing arguments down the logical stack, filling the remaining formal slots
with undefined, and preserving actual argc, function and new.target. It keeps
extra actual arguments and recognizes the zero-parameter no-adaptation sentinel.
The native adaptation test checks exact stack movement, receiver/argument order,
untouched call registers and a C++ FLUSHW probe across actual/formal counts up
to 255. A second native test jumps through several synthetic JS dispatch-table
entries and checks actual argc and new.target in the target code.

CallFunction loads the callee context, skips receiver conversion for strict or
native functions, replaces null/undefined with the global proxy, and uses ToObject
for primitive sloppy receivers. Invocation supports debugger call hooks with tagged
argument counts saved across the runtime call, and jumps/calls through the current
JS dispatch-table entry. The debugger hook and receiver-conversion paths compile
and now execute in real isolates. Frame restart reuses invocation with
argument adaptation disabled; NotifyDeoptimized restores the saved accumulator.

InterpreterEntryTrampoline now compiles the readonly jitless entry path: reset
SFI age, obtain BytecodeArray or InterpreterData bytecode, fall back to runtime
lazy compilation, select a feedback vector, build the interpreter frame and
initialize its register file. It initializes an incoming new.target/generator
register, performs native-spill-aware real/interrupt stack checks, dispatches
through the interpreter handler table and records the true continuation PC.
Returns drop the maximum of formal and actual argument counts; interrupts save
the function-entry offset and reload bytecode state after StackGuard.

The bytecode-offset helper has a native execution test covering unscaled, wide,
extra-wide and both debug-break prefixes, ordinary operand advancement,
Return/SuspendGenerator detection, and restoring the original prefixed JumpLoop
offset. The test supplies known bytecode size tables to isolate the architecture
logic; native d8 now executes real handlers as well.

Interpreter call/construct argument-transfer builtins now compile, including
implicit undefined receivers, final spread values, allocation-site feedback and
array-constructor dispatch. They copy descending interpreter-register arguments
to the ascending logical argument area, preserving the target, actual argc and
construct new.target. Their emitted code now executes in native d8; see the validation results above.

Interpreter re-entry now selects the default or profiling trampoline, reconstructs
the handler return link using the saved interpreter continuation offset minus
SPARC's eight-byte return adjustment, reloads the frame's bytecode state and
jumps through the dispatch table without changing the accumulator. Next-bytecode
re-entry handles the function-entry sentinel, advances through the tested size
logic, saves the new Smi offset and rejects an invalid return-bytecode advance.
A native test restores bytecode/offset from an interpreter-layout frame, enters
synthetic handlers through several table entries and returns to an explicit
continuation, checking accumulator and offset preservation. Real VM re-entry,
profiling trampoline selection and exception-driven dispatch remain unverified.

ConstructForwardAllArgs copies actual arguments from the current or parent frame,
pushes a receiver slot and tails to Construct after checking stack space. Bound
function call/construct helpers insert bound arguments before existing arguments,
retain the receiver, update actual argc and preserve construct new.target. Calls
replace this with BoundThis; construction substitutes the bound target only when
new.target equals the bound function. They reuse the native-tested raw argument
copy helper; full bound-function and forwarding semantics await VM execution.

Variable-argument call/construct builtins allocate room for appended arguments,
move the existing receiver/arguments to the lower logical stack, update argc,
and copy FixedArray elements with hole-to-undefined conversion. Forwarding
variants validate constructor new.target and copy the requested suffix of the
caller's actual arguments. Their shared movement helper has a native test that
checks overlap, exact SP movement, the first free slot, preserved target/list/
new.target registers and a C++ FLUSHW probe, with existing/extra counts up to 255.
Array-hole conversion, constructor validation and real caller-frame forwarding
still await execution in a V8 isolate.

The builtin and generic JavaScript construct stubs now build construct frames,
preserve original arguments/context, copy outgoing arguments, and invoke the
constructor with new.target. The generic stub allocates a receiver for base
constructors, uses the hole for derived constructors, selects an object result
or the saved receiver, and throws when a derived receiver remains uninitialized.
It records the construct-create continuation for deoptimization. The interpreter
fast-constructor entry uses the complete generic construction path, with its
shared descriptor and argument layout; no separate fast-construct frame is
needed for this jitless implementation. These construct paths still require
real VM execution, GC and constructor-semantics tests.

DoubleToI and the compiler's ArchTruncateDoubleToI instruction use an integer
IEEE-754 conversion: truncate the magnitude, retain the low 32 bits, apply the
sign and sign-extend the result. Negative exponents, NaNs/infinities and exponents
at least 84 yield zero. Reserved GP scratch registers preserve the JS register
set and FP input; there are no conversion instructions or runtime calls. Native
checks compare against V8's DoubleToInt32 oracle across all exponent encodings,
both signs, boundary fractions and 20,000 deterministic random patterns. The
same conversion supports both FP-register input and an in-place stack slot for
the builtin calling convention. Full compiler-generated builtin execution is
still pending.

Generator resumption now stores the input with a write barrier, restores the
suspended function/context and parameters/receiver, supports debugger stepping,
and dispatches with the generator object in new.target. The write-barrier helper
checks Smi and source/destination page flags, preserves descriptor registers and
the return link, and calls V8's existing RecordWrite builtin. Its native test
constructs real MemoryChunk headers in aligned storage, checks flag combinations
and Smi skips, and uses a synthetic callee that records object/slot/value and
clobbers descriptor registers to verify restoration. Real GC marking/remembered
sets and debugger stepping need dedicated stress coverage. Production
compilation succeeds and mksnapshot links.

API callback and named accessor/interceptor generators now compile and resolve
in mksnapshot. The shared API return path enters/restores HandleScope state,
selects profiling/side-effect thunks, reads the managed return-value slot,
normalizes the not-intercepted sentinel, removes handle-scope extensions when
needed and propagates scheduled exceptions. Calls use the existing GC-capable
native bridge with API exit-frame metadata. Setter/name Local arguments follow
the configured direct/indirect handle convention.

A native callback-layout test passes stack data to the public FunctionCallbackInfo
API, checks Length/GetIsolate/IsConstructCall and argument slots, verifies exit
metadata and the exact C return PC after FLUSHW, and writes Smi 42 through
GetReturnValue(). It exercises zero to 255 arguments and balances the caller
stack. It does not execute the complete new builtin generators: real handle-scope
extension cleanup, profiling thunks, accessor/interceptor calls, exceptions and
GC inside API callbacks remain unverified. mksnapshot now links with no unresolved builtin generators.

The four builtin-continuation entrypoints now restore the default configuration's
allocatable GP registers, untag JavaScript argc, install lazy results in the
code-stub or last JavaScript argument slot, restore caller FP/true PC and jump
through the Smi-indexed builtin table. Native checks construct continuation
frames for all four variants, exercise two distinct builtin indices and argument
counts through 64, inspect all restored registers and argument slots, and execute
the final return to the original continuation. The test driver links the real
register-configuration implementation. Integration with deoptimizer-produced
frames, GC and optimized JavaScript remains unverified.

DirectCEntry now has a native bridge for movable callers: g5 supplies the C
function and g4 its argument-slot count. The caller reserves its exit-frame PC
slot and outgoing stack slots. The bridge stores o7+8 in the GC-visible PC slot,
opens an aligned native window sized for stacked arguments, forwards six GP
registers plus stacked slots, preserves paired results and restores the exact
old SP. It reloads the caller PC from the slot before returning, so a GC update
can redirect the return. Native tests cover 0/6/8/256 arguments, both stack
alignments, FLUSHW, context restoration and a C callback that changes the return
slot to another continuation. This simulates a PC update; actual code movement
under V8 GC remains unverified.

The current port is restricted to the configured readonly V8_JITLESS build.
Eight baseline, OSR and deoptimizer entrypoints are guarded with explicit traps
for that configuration, and non-jitless generation is rejected. Their full JIT
implementations remain future work; they must not be counted as JIT support.
The complete continuation implementations described above are retained.

Running mjsunit on the T7 GYP build
----------------------------------

From /root/node-sparc64:

    python3 tools/sparc64/write-test-config.py
    cd deps/v8
    python3 -u tools/run-tests.py --outdir=../../out/Release \
      --variants=jitless --no-presubmit -j32 --progress=verbose \
      --exit-after-n-failures=0 \
      --json-test-results=/root/node-sparc64/out/sparc64-mjsunit.json mjsunit

The metadata helper derives enabled features from the Release GYP
definitions and rejects changes to the audited upstream metadata schema.
The runner applies upstream build/variant exclusions. Added exclusions
cover only the unsupported SPARC flush-to-zero helper and two Temporal
regressions when Temporal is disabled. Crashes and incorrect results in
applicable tests remain unexpected failures.

Node application milestone
--------------------------

Reached on T7, 2026-10-26. The current out/Release/node is a 57 MB jitless
Node v26.12.1 with npm 11.20.0, system OpenSSL 3.6.5 and ICU 78.3. It links
libssl.so.3, libcrypto.so.3, libicui18n.so.78, libicuuc.so.78 and
libicudata.so.78. No runtime JIT was enabled.

Build:

    sh tools/sparc64/configure-node.sh
    make -C out -j96 BUILDTYPE=Release node

Seven selected Node tests passed in the initial minimal build: Buffer float
reads/writes, filesystem promises, HTTP headers, ESM imports, worker data,
and child-process execFile. Each took under 0.5 seconds. That batch used
15-second per-test and 120-second overall limits. The minimal node/d8 and
configuration are preserved at out/sparc64-jitless-minimal/. The full V8
mjsunit result above covers that minimal configuration; the application
configuration has the targeted validation described here.

Reproduce the application checks:

    timeout 180 sh tools/sparc64/test-apps.sh

Validated with the application configuration:

- npm CLI and registry package installation over HTTPS. npm installed
  92 packages in 7 seconds; the final locked npm ci run took 8 seconds.
- Intl.NumberFormat using ICU.
- Express 5.3.0: verified localhost HTTPS, JSON request/response with
  Unicode payloads, and a known SHA-256 result.
- ws 8.22.0: secure WebSocket echo with payload and clean-close checks.
- http-server 14.1.1: the published CLI served a static file correctly.

The package manifest and lockfile are kept in tools/sparc64/apps/. The
script uses npm ci with install scripts and optional dependencies disabled,
creates a short-lived localhost test certificate, and checks its trust and
hostname normally. Application deadlines are 20 seconds, package
installation is limited to 90 seconds, and the overall command is capped
at 180 seconds. No benchmark, pummel, long stress loop or full Node suite
was run for this milestone.

Logs: out/sparc64-node-configure.log, out/sparc64-node-build.log,
out/sparc64-node-first-start.log, out/sparc64-node-core-smoke.log, and
out/sparc64-node-app-smoke.log.

Remaining limitations: runtime JIT and WebAssembly are disabled, as are
Temporal, FFI, bundled Amaro/TypeScript stripping, the Node startup
snapshot and Node code cache. A bounded native Node-API addon/GC check now passes; broader third-party
addons and Node conformance/stress have not been validated. This is evidence that useful
applications run, not a claim of full production readiness. Future V8
test runs should regenerate metadata for the current feature build with
python3 tools/sparc64/write-test-config.py. All changes remain local and
uncommitted.

Pre-Sparkplug checkpoint and readiness review
-------------------------------------------

The bounded native-addon check passed on T7:

    timeout 65 sh tools/sparc64/test-addon-gc.sh

It builds a small shared Node-API addon against this checkout's headers,
without node-gyp downloads. Ten native callbacks include nested JS/native
reentry and exception propagation. Sixteen synchronous major collections
request compaction with --compact-on-every-full-gc, and six minor
collections exercise live objects and new fields after promotion. Native
register windows are flushed around callbacks; strong references, local
handles, escaped results, original exception identity and named JS stack
frames are checked. The test allocates enough native handles to span
handle blocks and uses six outer rounds. The JS portion took 409 ms.
Compilation and execution each have a 30-second limit. Evidence:
out/sparc64-addon-gc.log. This does not validate movement or patching of
new JIT InstructionStreams; that is a separate acceptance gate below.

Review findings and inventory:

    python3 tools/sparc64/write-test-config.py
    timeout 75 python3 tools/sparc64/inventory-jit.py

The inventory uses the native Release preprocessor definitions and include
paths, expanding opcode macros and filtering selector definitions by the
current build. It records 225 emitted cases out of 489 declared opcodes,
264 declarations without an emitter, twenty compiled whole-body selector
guards, and the eight guarded builtins. The missing declarations include
SIMD, optional operators, optimizer operations and alternate atomic
opcodes; they are not all required for Sparkplug. Intentional guards
remain valid when machine capability flags force earlier lowering.
Conditional guards inside method bodies are not included in that count.
Output: out/sparc64-jit-inventory.json.

The two baseline architecture files are absent:

- deps/v8/src/baseline/sparc64/baseline-assembler-sparc64-inl.h
- deps/v8/src/baseline/sparc64/baseline-compiler-sparc64-inl.h

Both shared baseline architecture include switches lack SPARC64 branches.
The SPARC64 BUILD.gn header list and GYP architecture selection also need
baseline entries. Reference the PPC and s390 baseline files for big-endian
contracts, RISC-V for explicit instruction sequences, and x64 for shared
baseline behavior and tests. Adapt their ABI assumptions to SPARC.

Guarded builtin split:

| Builtin | Initial Sparkplug requirement |
| --- | --- |
| BaselineOutOfLinePrologue | Implement entry, frame and feedback setup |
| BaselineOutOfLinePrologueDeopt | Implement baseline-entry fallback |
| InterpreterOnStackReplacement_ToBaseline | Implement interpreter-to-baseline frame conversion |
| BaselineOnStackReplacement | Optimizer OSR; keep unreachable while optimizing tiers are disabled |
| InterpreterOnStackReplacement | Optimizer OSR; keep unreachable while optimizing tiers are disabled |
| DeoptimizationEntry_Eager | Optimizing-tier register/frame reconstruction |
| DeoptimizationEntry_Lazy | Optimizing-tier register/frame reconstruction |
| DeoptimizationEntry_LazyAfterFastCall | Optimizing-tier register/frame reconstruction |

The current GenerateJitlessTrap helper fatally rejects non-jitless snapshot
generation. Its treatment of unused optimizer builtins must be split from
required baseline implementations before enabling Sparkplug. Any retained
trap needs a verified disabled-feature gate; active paths must be real
implementations. The baseline prologue fallback is required even when
optimized-code deoptimization is disabled.

InterpreterAssembler::OnStackReplacement can select cached baseline code
independently of optimizer OSR. --no-use-osr alone does not establish that
InterpreterOnStackReplacement_ToBaseline is unreachable. Implement and
validate that path before ordinary baseline loop workloads.

Sparkplug implementation checklist, in dependency order:

1. [ ] Use a separate JIT checkout/build populated from the checkpoint.
   Preserve the runnable jitless node/d8 and their configuration.
2. [ ] Add the two SPARC baseline headers, shared include branches, and
   GN/GYP source/header wiring, following PPC/s390. Add a separate build
   recipe that omits --v8-lite-mode, enables Sparkplug, and explicitly
   keeps Maglev, runtime TurboFan and WebAssembly disabled. Do not change
   the working jitless configure recipes.
3. [ ] Implement BaselineAssembler's required interface: register/frame
   operands, moves, scratch scopes, loads/stores and write barriers,
   comparisons/branches, pushes/pops, builtin/runtime calls, interrupt
   budget checks, returns and switch dispatch. The inventory includes
   the PPC architecture's method names as a reference, not an ABI template.
4. [ ] Reserve scratch registers consistently with MacroAssembler's
   implicit g1..g5 use. Preserve g6/g7, native FP i6, V8 FP l5, root l7
   and context l6. Keep actual argc/receiver and argument-adaptation
   conventions consistent with the existing entry and call builtins.
5. [ ] Implement baseline compiler prologue/frame filling and frame-size
   verification. Native SP is biased by 2047; logical V8 SP adds another
   176 bytes. FP-based V8 slots already use unbiased l5. Lay out bytecode,
   feedback-cell/vector and register-file slots using the shared baseline
   constants, and handle new.target/generator registers and stack limits.
6. [ ] Implement the three baseline-required builtins above. Audit shared
   function info, feedback-vector, JS dispatch installation, code flushing
   and interpreter fallback for baseline Code objects, rather than assuming
   only BytecodeArray/InterpreterData in the jitless paths.
7. [ ] Regenerate a non-jitless snapshot with optimizing JS tiers disabled.
   Fix additional scalar emitters actually reached by that configuration.
   Builtins still use the compiler backend even without runtime TurboFan;
   the remaining backend inventory cannot all be deferred automatically.
8. [ ] Compile and execute one small function, prove it is baseline code
   using V8's test intrinsics/tier traces, and compare its result with
   Ignition. Start with straight-line code, then calls/returns/branches.
9. [ ] Validate loop backedges and interpreter-to-baseline OSR, argument
   counts, construction, exceptions, stack traces and runtime/API calls.
   A CALL link is instruction PC; saved return PCs are PC+8. Every control
   transfer needs the correct delay slot.
10. [ ] Check executable-memory permissions and instruction-cache flushing
    for newly generated and patched code. CpuFeatures currently uses
    __builtin___clear_cache; test actual execution after patching. Check
    relocations and literal slots in heap-allocated Code/InstructionStream
    objects, including PC updates while GC moves generated code.
11. [ ] Address long-branch reach before general baseline workloads. The
    current assembler checks branch displacement bounds but has no long
    branch trampoline. Preserve constant/literal alignment and big-endian
    raw bits, and retain explicit failures for unsupported cases.
12. [ ] Run focused baseline mjsunit cases and the bounded addon/GC and
    application smoke checks with the new tier, keeping the jitless build
    as the reference. Expand coverage when a concrete failure or new
    implementation justifies it. Optimizing tiers, their deoptimization
    entries and full backend/FP C-linkage coverage are later milestones.

Checkpoint creation on T7:

    python3 tools/sparc64/checkpoint.py

The tool creates out/checkpoints/pre-sparkplug-<UTC timestamp>/ and writes
its path to out/sparc64-prejit-checkpoint-path.txt. It copies node/d8
without hard links, the compiled addon, config.gypi and V8 test metadata,
validation logs and the machine inventory. It records shared-library
dependencies rather than bundling OpenSSL/ICU. The source overlay contains
all changed tracked files and non-ignored untracked files; BASE_HEAD and
tracked.patch identify the base and provide a reviewable tracked diff.
manifest.json records source/artifact SHA-256 hashes, and SHA256SUMS
verifies every copied artifact. The tool rejects source changes during
checkpoint creation and creates no Git commit.

Recovery: create a clean checkout/worktree of BASE_HEAD and extract
source-overlay.tar.gz at its root. All current changes are additions or
modifications, so no deletion step is needed. tracked.patch is for review
or for applying tracked changes instead of the tracked overlay contents;
do not apply both to the same files. Use the saved configure script to
regenerate build files; config.gypi records the exact previous choices.
Verify SHA256SUMS before using saved binaries. The checkpoint is mirrored
to the local checkout's out/checkpoints/ directory for recovery.

Sparkplug development configuration
----------------------------------

The JIT checkout was created from the verified pre-Sparkplug checkpoint,
without changing the preserved jitless sources or binaries. Changes remain
local and uncommitted.

    sh tools/sparc64/configure-sparkplug.sh
    make -C out -j96 BUILDTYPE=Release d8 node

This configuration enables Sparkplug and disables runtime TurboFan,
Maglev, WebAssembly, Temporal, Node snapshots/code cache, Amaro and FFI.
It uses system OpenSSL and ICU. Regular expressions remain interpreted;
the SPARC native regexp backend is a separate task.

The SPARC baseline assembler/compiler implement frame/register access,
scratch scopes, loads/stores and write barriers, comparisons, control flow,
calls, interrupt budgets, returns, frame filling and switch dispatch.
Scratch registers i3..i5 avoid MacroAssembler's implicit g1..g5 temporaries.
Three baseline entrypoints now implement frame setup, entry fallback and
interpreter-to-baseline OSR. Cached baseline code is installed through
Runtime::kInstallBaselineCode before entry, including dispatch-table updates.
In the preserved Sparkplug checkpoint, the five optimizer-only entrypoints
retain checked guards while their compilers are disabled.

Baseline branches use CALL's 30-bit PC-relative displacement. The sequence
saves o7 in g4 and restores o7 in the delay slot, allowing wide forward
branches and loop backedges without losing the return link. Switches use
a compare chain; optimizing that dispatch is later work. The optimizing
compiler backend still has its original short-branch limitations.

Linux SPARC can return mmap addresses above the VA hole, such as
0xfff8000108741021 for a tagged Code pointer. JS dispatch entries pack code
pointers into 48 bits; that address would decode as 0x108741021. The native
allocator now uses 40-bit page-aligned random hints and unmaps/retries
private allocations whose full range cannot fit below 2^48. Four rejected
placements return ENOMEM. This preserves the existing dispatch-table format.
The standalone address test checks null, high and random hints, alignment,
read/write access, freeing the resulting allocations and rejecting an
oversized reservation.

Bounded validation commands:

    sh tools/sparc64/test-address-space.sh
    sh tools/sparc64/test-sparkplug.sh
    timeout 130 python3 tools/sparc64/test-sparkplug-upstream.py
    timeout 600 python3 tools/sparc64/test-assembler.py --full
    timeout 65 sh tools/sparc64/test-addon-gc.sh
    timeout 180 sh tools/sparc64/test-apps.sh

The upstream helper runs eight unmodified baseline cases with their own
Flags comments: general baseline behavior, modules, cross-realm calls,
load-IC transitions, batch compilation, baseline/bytecode flushing,
baseline-only flushing, and source positions. Three optimizer-OSR cases
require disabled optimizing tiers and are outside this selection. The
custom smoke test asserts actual baseline execution and covers calls,
arguments, branches, loops, exceptions, compaction, interpreter-to-baseline
OSR and an 18,000-statement loop with wide backedges.

The full applicable mjsunit suite runs with --variants=nooptimization, which
disables optimizing compilers while retaining Sparkplug. The runner omits
the variant's Wasm-only lazy-compilation flag in builds without Wasm.
The previous 6,412-case result belongs to the preserved jitless build.
The results in this section describe the preserved Sparkplug checkout.

Sparkplug validation on T7, 2026-10-26:

- 94/94 native encoder and MacroAssembler tests passed (104 ms runtime).
- 8/8 selected upstream baseline cases passed.
- Custom baseline-tier, wide-backedge, compaction and baseline-OSR checks passed.
- Native page allocation regression passed, including a forced high hint and
  an oversized reservation. The null-hint case failed before the allocator fix.
- 7/7 targeted Node core tests passed with --always-sparkplug: Buffer float
  reads/writes, HTTP headers, fs promises, ESM, worker data and child execFile.
- The worker case passed 20/20 additional runs, four processes at a time,
  with --always-sparkplug and per-process 15-second limits.
