TurboFan first execution milestone
---------------------------------

The separate node-sparc64-turbofan checkout and sparc64-turbofan branch start
from the verified Sparkplug source overlay. The parent checkpoint and its
manifest digest are recorded in out/sparc64-turbofan-parent.json. Changes
remain local and uncommitted.

    sh tools/sparc64/configure-turbofan.sh
    make -C out --eval=.SECONDARY: -j96 BUILDTYPE=Release d8
    python3 tools/sparc64/write-test-config.py --allow-turbofan
    sh tools/sparc64/test-turbofan.sh
    python3 tools/sparc64/test-turbofan-upstream.py

This enables TurboFan while retaining the Sparkplug configuration's other
feature restrictions. The five optimizer entrypoints now have implementations:
eager/lazy deoptimization, lazy deoptimization after a fast API call, and
interpreter/baseline optimizing OSR. Deoptimization saves the allocatable
register state, copies input/output frames, calls the shared deoptimizer and
resumes its continuation. It preserves the native register window and g6/g7,
uses logical V8 stack addresses and converts CALL links to return PCs with +8.
Stack iterability is updated with byte stores to the isolate field.

The baseline assembler loads cached OSR CodeWrapper entries through weak
references, rejects code marked for deoptimization and clears invalid slots.
The optimizing frame constructor records its OSR entry and allocates only the
additional frame slots. The backend now emits ArchCallJSFunction through the
dispatch table. External references to isolate fields use root-relative
addresses, following PPC and s390.

The custom test checks the executing TurboFan frame bit inside each optimized
function. It covers an eager type-change bailout, a lazy bailout with live
objects and GC, and floating-point locals surviving lazy deoptimization and
GC. A separate process disables Sparkplug and verifies interpreter-to-TurboFan
OSR. Each process has a 15-second limit. A preserved Sparkplug binary is a
negative control: it fails the TurboFan tier assertion.

Five selected upstream cases require observed optimizing compilation as well
as a successful exit: simple eager and lazy deoptimization, deoptimization
followed by GC, deoptimization with try/catch and baseline-to-TurboFan OSR.
The last case also asserts the executing tier after each transition.

Validation on T7, 2026-10-26: the custom tier, eager/lazy/object/double/GC
checks and direct interpreter OSR passed; 5/5 upstream optimizing cases,
8/8 upstream baseline cases, the wide-backedge/compaction/baseline-OSR smoke,
and 94/94 native encoder/MacroAssembler cases passed. The negative control
rejected the preserved non-optimizing binary at its executing-tier assertion.

These are bounded d8 checks. Full optimizing suites, broad arithmetic and
deoptimization coverage, concurrent compilation and the fast API exception
continuation still need validation. Node has not been built with this
experimental configuration; the working Node/application results belong to
the preserved Sparkplug checkout. Maglev, Wasm and native regexp compilation
remain separate work. The backend follow-up below removes the short-branch
limits recorded at this first milestone.

Final logs use the sparc64-turbofan-* prefix under out/. Preserve the d8-only
milestone separately:

    python3 tools/sparc64/checkpoint.py --profile turbofan

The manifest explicitly records the limited validation scope and includes
the source overlay, d8, configuration, parent checkpoint and validation logs.
Its path is written to out/sparc64-turbofan-checkpoint-path.txt.

Ordinary JavaScript backend and branch validation
------------------------------------------------

The backend follow-up adds 32/64-bit rotation, register byte reversal and
reversed loads/stores. Reversed non-atomic memory operations tolerate
unaligned DataView addresses; atomic operations retain their aligned load or
store and barriers. The native tests cover fixed bit patterns, aliased
registers and shift-count wrapping.

Label jumps now use CALL's 30-bit displacement while restoring o7 in the
delay slot. Conditional integer and floating-point branches invert a short
condition around that jump. Switch dispatch uses the same mechanism.
The reach covers the signed-int code-buffer offsets used by the assembler.
Native tests exercise both outcomes beyond the 19-bit conditional range,
including NaN comparisons, and forward/backward jumps and deoptimization
patches beyond the 22-bit unconditional range. Restoring the old conditional
emitter in a control build fails the new test at its displacement check.

Fast C calls reserve a three-instruction patch site at the native return PC.
Their lazy deoptimization entry restores the native register window and
argument area itself: normal cleanup code can already have been zapped when
the fast callback deoptimizes its caller. The native frame keeps the original
SP and stack-argument count in l4/l2. The exception path preserves V8 registers
without pushing the biased native i6 as a potential tagged heap pointer.

Constructor deoptimization now has a FAST_CONSTRUCT return landing with the
recorded invoke offset and receiver/primitive-result handling. Float32 spill
translation reads the first word of its slot, matching PPC/s390 and SPARC's
big-endian FP stores. These changes fixed the constructor, fast-API and
40-live-Float32 failures found by the first full optimizer run.

Validation on T7, 2026-10-26:

- Full applicable default-variant mjsunit: 6,539 cases, zero unexpected failures.
  This uses normal automatic/concurrent optimization settings. Two mixed
  JS/Wasm tests are excluded by the existing not-has-webassembly condition;
  their assertions are unchanged. The earlier full run exposed 14 applicable
  failures; all 14 passed after the fixes.
- Expanded arithmetic/call/data-access selection: 32/32 passed.
- Native encoder/MacroAssembler/backend tests: 100/100 passed, including all
  earlier calling-convention checks and the long-branch controls.
- Directed optimized arithmetic/data-access matrix: 71 edge-case vectors,
  with BigInt integer references, overflow, signed zero, NaN, infinities,
  subnormals, Float32 conversion, unaligned little/big-endian DataView
  integer/BigInt reads and integer/BigInt/float stores.
- A real optimized function generated 1,120,356 machine-code bytes and passed
  an eager type-change bailout across the old conditional-branch limit. It
  has 4,000 call sites with surplus arguments. Its deoptimization count stays
  below V8's architecture-independent 16,384-entry limit.
- Node now builds with TurboFan and passes the forced executing-tier,
  eager/lazy deoptimization, live object/double and GC checks. A separate
  arithmetic function reaches TurboFan through normal concurrent tiering;
  its results are checked against BigInt arithmetic.

Reproduce the focused checks after building node/d8:

    out/Release/d8 --allow-natives-syntax --no-concurrent-recompilation \
      tools/sparc64/test-backend.js
    python3 tools/sparc64/test-backend-large.py
    out/Release/node --allow-natives-syntax --expose-gc \
      tools/sparc64/test-node-turbofan.cjs
    python3 tools/sparc64/test-assembler.py --full

The full mjsunit command is the upstream tools/run-tests.py runner from
deps/v8 with --outdir=../../out/Release --variants=default --no-presubmit
-j16 -t15 --exit-after-n-failures=0 --rerun-failures-count=0, selecting mjsunit.
Save JSON results with --json-test-results and require a nonzero test count.

Backend audit: 234/489 declared emitter cases are implemented. This count
does not measure JavaScript completeness. Remaining declarations include
disabled Wasm/SIMD and reference atomics, sandbox/pointer-compression paths,
optional write-barrier verification, 32-bit paired operations and unused
PPC-style opcode forms. SPARC's selector emits its own load/store opcodes for
ordinary numeric atomics rather than the unused generic atomic declarations.
Only kWord32ShiftIsSafe is advertised as an optional machine capability;
unsupported rounding/popcount operations use shared lowering. Raw Float32
overflow conversions and stack-return Peek forms belong to other lowering
and ABI paths, rather than ordinary JS number conversion or JS register
returns. The selector retains the same unimplemented Float64 insertion and
Float16 raw-conversion hooks as the PPC reference. Enabling further features
requires a separate audit and tests; these declarations are not silently
treated as implemented.

These results complete the ordinary-JS backend, branch-reach and broad
arithmetic/data-access/call validation milestone for this configuration.
Broader GC/deoptimization stress, additional V8 conformance suites, Node core
and application coverage, feature restoration and sustained reliability work
remain. The older Node application results still describe Sparkplug; this
stage verifies optimized execution inside Node itself.

Preserve this stage with python3 tools/sparc64/checkpoint.py --profile backend.
It includes both node and d8, the full-suite results and focused evidence.

TurboFan runtime stress and application validation
-------------------------------------------------

The runtime validation build keeps the same feature configuration and adds
VERIFY_HEAP. Heap verification is a build capability; the earlier binary's
read-only false flag could not validate heap pointers at collection time.

    sh tools/sparc64/configure-turbofan-stress.sh
    make -C out -j96 BUILDTYPE=Release d8 node
    python3 tools/sparc64/write-test-config.py --allow-turbofan
    python3 -u tools/sparc64/test-turbofan-runtime.py

The runtime runner executes four independent groups, recording commands,
timeouts, build metadata and before/after node/d8 SHA-256 hashes. Each group
can be rerun with --group stress, core, applications or directed.

The upstream stress selection covers deoptimization, escape analysis and
materialization, constructor inlining, OSR/try-catch, fast native API calls
and live Float32 spills. Four profiles request compaction, incremental
marking, concurrent allocation and concurrent inlining, all with --verify-heap.
The directed GC test checks optimized entries, old-to-young object/array
stores, live objects/doubles, eager/lazy invalidation and exceptions across
collections. Optimized addon reentry additionally keeps native handles and
JS state live through major/minor GC and lazy deoptimization.
The probes record executing-tier status unconditionally. The addon fixture
reoptimizes before deliberate invalidation: GC may already have revoked the
previous optimized caller when clearing weak field-type dependencies.

Node's upstream buffer, stream, network, TLS, worker and child-process tests
run through tools/test.py. The runner records four explicit exclusions:
two Wasm message-port tests and two TLS fetch tests. Undici's HTTP/1 parser
requires Wasm, so global fetch over HTTP/1 remains unavailable in this build;
the node:http/node:https clients and the tested npm/application paths work
without it. Existing test cases and assertions are unchanged. The common
harness recognizes sparc64 as 64-bit, enabling the over-4-GB buffer check
that its old architecture list incorrectly skipped.

The initial Node run exposed SIGBUS in BigInt serialization, shared by
worker primitive errors and advanced child-process IPC. GDB identified an
unaligned STX into the serialized byte buffer. Both big-endian serialization
and deserialization now use the existing base unaligned-access helpers for
that buffer. The wire format stays little-endian. The directed regression
checks byte order independently and varies internal padding and input-buffer
alignment, including large signed BigInts, maps and structuredClone.

Published application checks install the locked dependencies with npm ci,
exercise Express HTTPS/JSON, WebSocket echo and the http-server CLI. A local
npm fixture also checks lifecycle scripts, packing, offline installation,
clean installation and execution of an installed command. The
two-minute soak uses 16 concurrent HTTPS requests, text/binary WebSockets,
two worker isolates and repeated minor/compacting major collections. It
checks response values against BigInt references, requires actual automatic
TurboFan execution in the Express handler and workers, and records eight
post-GC memory samples. After warmup it rejects retained-heap growth of
12 MiB or RSS growth of 96 MiB; those bounded checks do not establish
long-term leak freedom.

The runtime checkpoint profile requires passing evidence for all four groups,
matching binary hashes, nonzero suite counts and the complete soak:

    python3 tools/sparc64/checkpoint.py --profile runtime

It preserves node, d8, the addon, build configuration, source overlay,
validation evidence and the preceding backend checkpoint's manifest. Its
path is written to out/sparc64-runtime-checkpoint-path.txt. Broader Node
suites, long-duration reliability and restoration of disabled features are
separate work.

Final validation on T7, 2026-10-26, using the same node/d8 binary hashes:

- Upstream heap-verified V8 stress: 49 compaction, 49 incremental-marking,
  49 concurrent-allocation and 48 concurrent-inlining cases; 195 passed,
  zero unexpected failures.
- Node core selection: 988 passed, seven standard upstream skips and four
  explicit disabled-Wasm exclusions. Both previously crashing serialization
  cases and the over-4-GB buffer case pass with their original assertions.
- Directed GC/deoptimization: three profiles, each with 90 verified optimized
  entries and 300 explicit collections; all pass with --verify-heap.
  Interpreter OSR, normal concurrent Node tiering and baseline regression
  checks also pass.
- Native addon: 24 verified optimized caller entries with 48 major/minor GC
  pairs, including explicit lazy invalidation during native callback reentry.
  The earlier nested-callback/exception/handle test also passes.
- BigInt serialization: 2,729 independent endian, buffer-alignment, object/map
  round-trip and structuredClone checks pass.
- npm lifecycle/pack/local-install/clean-install/executable workflows and
  Express HTTPS/JSON, secure WebSockets and published http-server checks pass.
- Heap-verified soak: 121.47 seconds, 14,848 HTTPS requests, 928 WebSocket
  exchanges and 15,923,000 checked worker calculations. The Express handler
  recorded 1,697 actual TurboFan entries; both worker isolates also proved
  automatic optimized execution. From the third post-GC sample to the last,
  retained JS heap decreased 59,176 bytes and RSS increased 5,103,616 bytes.

The runtime audit checks every group's success markers and zero exit status
against its logs, and verifies matching current binary hashes. Final directed
results supersede the initial custom-probe failures; no engine change was
needed for those probe corrections. The architecture fix from this milestone
is the unaligned serialized BigInt buffer access described above.

Node embedded snapshots and code caching
----------------------------------------

This stage uses the separate sparc64-snapshots branch and
node-sparc64-snapshots worktree on both hosts. The preceding runtime build
and its checkpoint remain in node-sparc64-turbofan. The parent manifest is
copied to out/sparc64-snapshot-parent.json after verification of all 120 port
source files and the node/d8 binary hashes.

The initial audit found that user-created snapshots and VM cached-data APIs
already pass the selected upstream tests on the parent binary. The disabled
features are Node's embedded bootstrapped startup snapshot and builtin-module
cache. The new configure recipe enables both while retaining TurboFan,
Sparkplug, heap-verification support and the existing disabled-feature set.

    sh tools/sparc64/configure-snapshots.sh
    make -C out -j96 BUILDTYPE=Release d8 node
    python3 tools/sparc64/write-test-config.py --allow-turbofan
    python3 -u tools/sparc64/test-snapshot-upstream.py
    python3 -u tools/sparc64/test-snapshot-state.py

The upstream selection covers startup snapshots, VM caches and module compile
caches, including worker cache tests and the no-snapshot fallback. Standard
upstream skips remain visible in the recorded TAP and JSON results. No test
assertions are changed. The directed probe checks actual compiled-in-snapshot
and compiled-with-cache bookkeeping, rather than only configure variables.

The state fixture serializes cyclic objects, BigInts, maps and typed arrays;
three fresh processes restore them and verify GC, TurboFan execution and
deoptimization. The snapshot builder does not install global gc(), so this
fixture uses the existing V8 CollectGarbage testing intrinsic. The code-cache
fixture uses separate processes for production, consumption, corrupt-header,
source, version and flag mismatch rejection, avoiding isolate-local compilation
cache hits that could bypass input-cache validation.

Code caches preserve compilation data such as bytecode, not a previously
optimized JavaScript machine-code activation. The restored functions are
explicitly compiled and executed with TurboFan after loading.

Repeat the runtime/application checks on the new binary with
test-turbofan-runtime.py --group core, --group directed and --group applications.
After builds and concurrent test jobs finish, compare startup times:

    python3 tools/sparc64/benchmark-startup.py

It measures fresh-process startup with warm filesystem caches, interleaving
15 samples per workload/mode after warmup. It compares the preserved parent,
the new default and the new --no-node-snapshot fallback, for empty startup
and a builtin-module workload. It records raw samples, medians, host load
and binary hashes; it does not drop shared filesystem caches.

The snapshots checkpoint profile requires matching binary hashes for the
snapshot/cache checks, core/application/directed regressions and benchmark.
It includes node, d8, node_mksnapshot, generated node_snapshot.cc, the directed
snapshot/cache artifacts, toolchain information and the verified parent manifest:

    python3 tools/sparc64/checkpoint.py --profile snapshots

The resulting pointer is out/sparc64-snapshots-checkpoint-path.txt.

Final validation on T7, 2026-10-26:

- Embedded startup snapshot and builtin cache generation/linking succeeded.
  The runtime probe records 82 modules restored from the snapshot and 73
  compiled with cache, with zero compiled without cache. Its no-snapshot
  control records zero snapshot/cache hits and 147 compilations without cache.
- Upstream selection: 59 main-thread cases and three worker cases, zero
  failures. Three main-thread Amaro-dependent cases skip normally. The
  pre-existing global skip for test-snapshot-incompatible remains unchanged;
  this milestone does not claim that skipped scenario. Snapshot reproducibility,
  the TypeScript compiler snapshot fixture and builtin cache assertions pass.
- All 12 directed state/cache checks pass, including three independent state
  restorations with actual TurboFan execution, GC and eager deoptimization.
  Corrupt, source, version and flag mismatch caches are rejected and execution
  falls back correctly.
- Node core regressions: 988 passed, seven standard upstream skips and the
  same four explicit disabled-Wasm exclusions as the parent build.
- Optimized GC/deoptimization, addon reentry, OSR, baseline, serialization,
  npm lifecycle/install/pack/executable and published application checks pass.
- The heap-verified application soak passes: 15,632 HTTPS requests and 977
  WebSocket exchanges over 120.63 seconds, with actual automatic TurboFan
  execution in the handler and both worker isolates. Post-warmup retained
  heap growth is under 0.2 MiB and RSS decreases by 3.125 MiB.

Median fresh-process startup on T7, warm filesystem caches, 15 interleaved
samples per cell after warmup:

| Workload | Preserved parent | New default | New --no-node-snapshot |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Empty startup | 371.98 ms | 108.35 ms | 304.99 ms |
| Builtin imports | 418.90 ms | 151.43 ms | 437.05 ms |

The new default is about 3.4x faster for empty startup and 2.8x for the builtin
workload in this measurement. These are warm-filesystem results on the loaded
T7, not cold-disk startup or steady-state application benchmarks. Raw samples,
load averages and binary hashes are retained with the results.

No further SPARC backend change was required for this stage. The restoration
uses the new configure recipe and upstream snapshot/cache implementations;
the working parent sources and binary hashes remain unchanged.


Native RegExp checkpoint (2026-10-28)
------------------------------------

The current development worktree is `node-sparc64-regexp`, branch
`sparc64-regexp`, based on the verified 126-file snapshot/TurboFan checkpoint
`node-snapshots-20261026T212627.382040Z`. The snapshot worktree and its live
Node/d8 binaries remain unchanged. This stage enables native RegExp compilation
by default alongside Sparkplug, TurboFan, embedded startup snapshots and builtin
code cache. Wasm, Maglev, Temporal, Amaro and FFI remain disabled.

The new `RegExpMacroAssemblerSPARC64` follows the RISC-V scalar backend and the
PPC big-endian character-loading conventions. It uses SPARC register windows for
native entry, keeps i6 as the native linkage and l5 as the V8 frame pointer,
uses aligned native frames, and passes moving-code stack-guard callbacks through
the existing immovable DirectCEntry builtin. Backtrack targets and saved internal
return PCs are code-relative. Captures/register slots are stored as full 64-bit
values; output captures and backtrack-stack entries use 32-bit values. Unaligned
subject reads and SIMD scanning are disabled. The assembler now supports label
code offsets through forward fixups, including after buffer growth.

Validation on T7, GCC 16.2.1:

- Full default mjsunit: 6,544 cases, zero unexpected results. Five previously
  disabled SPARC interpreter-only exclusions were removed; assertions were not
  changed.
- RegExp selection: 109 default cases, 103 forced-JIT cases and 103 forced-JIT
  compaction/heap-verification cases, all passing. The native profiles use V8's
  stress_regexp_jit variant. `regexp-cons-empty-first` explicitly requires the
  interpreter, so it runs in the default profile only. Upstream variant/feature
  exclusions remain in effect; exact commands and selections are recorded.
- Differential corpus: 70,568 exec cases plus replace, split and matchAll results
  agree across interpreter, direct native compilation and normal tier-up. Code
  presence together with bytecode absence proves native compilation; the
  interpreter negative control reports zero native cases. The code-presence
  intrinsic alone also counts interpreter trampolines and is insufficient.
- A 40,001-character capturing match invokes GrowStack ten times and returns
  correct captures, verified with a debugger breakpoint and a native-code check.
- Native-addon interruption: ten Latin-1/UTF-16 matches per configuration survive
  a full compacting GC and nested matching, then terminate as requested. Both
  normal snapshot/tier-up and fresh-bootstrap/direct-native configurations pass
  with heap verification. The surviving regexps remain usable afterward.
- Native assembler suite: 101/101, including forward/backward label offsets over
  more than 2 MiB and buffer growth.
- Existing snapshot/cache state suite: all 12 cases pass, including actual builtin
  cache usage, restored state, TurboFan/deoptimization and cache rejection controls.
- Node core: 995 selected cases, 989 passes, six normal upstream skips, zero
  failures, with four explicit disabled-Wasm exclusions. Directed optimized
  GC/deopt, native-addon, serialization, OSR and baseline checks pass.
- npm install/run/pack/offline local install/ci/exec, Express HTTPS, ws and
  http-server pass. The 120.49-second application soak served 23,552 HTTPS
  requests with actual optimized execution in the handler and both workers.

Five interleaved fresh-process samples per mode, 100,000 matches per workload,
produced these native/interpreter median speedups on the loaded T7:

| Workload | Speedup |
| --- | --- |
| Route captures | 3.05x |
| Email validation | 5.06x |
| Log search | 3.56x |
| Unicode letter matching | 1.26x |
| Case-insensitive backreference | 7.61x |

All checksums agree. These are bounded microbenchmarks; the application soak is
validation, not a controlled application performance comparison.

One debug configuration needs care: `--verify-heap --no-regexp-tier-up` with the
embedded Node snapshot trips the shared V8 tick-counter verifier on RegExp data
serialized with tier-up enabled. Normal snapshot/tier-up heap verification
passes. The direct-native GC control uses `--no-node-snapshot`, which creates the
initial RegExp data with matching flags; it also passes. No verifier assertion
was weakened. This flag/snapshot compatibility issue remains separate from the
native matching implementation.

