Optimized Node conformance and application hardening (2026-10-28)
---------------------------------------------------------------

The current worktree is `node-sparc64-hardening`, branch `sparc64-hardening`.
It starts from the verified 142-file native RegExp checkpoint
`node-regexp-20261028T131434.169010Z`; that worktree, its source overlay and its
live Node/d8 binaries remain unchanged. Node is still v26.12.1, with Sparkplug,
TurboFan, native RegExp, startup snapshot and builtin cache enabled. Wasm,
Maglev, Temporal, Amaro and FFI remain disabled.

Two runtime fixes were required:

- RegExp data restored from a startup snapshot now initializes its tier-up
  counter from the consumer flags, using the same rule as the factory. A
  snapshot produced with tier-up enabled can therefore be used with
  `--verify-heap --no-regexp-tier-up`, zero ticks, a different tick budget or
  interpreter-only execution. The earlier debug-flag caveat is resolved.
  Experimental RegExp data and user-code cache state are preserved.
- The inspector's big-endian UTF-16LE conversion now uses the existing
  unaligned-safe little-endian read helper. CBOR strings may start at odd
  addresses; the former typed load raised SIGBUS. This caused REPL history
  preview and inspector Unicode evaluation to crash. No verifier or test
  assertion was weakened.

The first broad baseline selected 5,832 cases and recorded 50 failures. After
fixes and evidence-based environment/feature classification, the final runs
produced zero unexpected results:

| Selection | Cases | Passes | Upstream skips |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| parallel, sequential, async-hooks, ESM, module-hooks, message, report, trace_events, test-runner, node-api | 5,790 | 5,354 | 436 |
| addons and js-native-api | 137 | 133 | 4 |
| abort, pseudo-tty, SEA and SQLite | 93 | 55 | 38 |
| Native C++ | 280 | 280 | 0 |

That is 5,542 Node test passes and 280 C++ test passes. Shared-library SEA
restrictions and other existing feature/platform skips remain visible in the
raw TAP output. A single additional C++ test executes WebAssembly and is
explicitly excluded. Forty-five named JavaScript exclusions and their reasons
are in `hardening-exclusions.json`; these are not extra passing cases.

Most exclusions require disabled Wasm, including HTTP/1 fetch and builtin
WebSocket operations through Undici; one requires Amaro TypeScript stripping.
Three independent controls explain the remaining platform/tool cases:

- `O_NOATIME` is 0x200000 on SPARC Linux. The Node value equals a compiled C
  program using the native headers; the upstream test assumes x86's 0x40000.
- A permission-denied deletion test assumes non-root execution. Its unchanged
  source passes when staged and run as `nobody`; the root baseline bypasses
  the intended filesystem restriction.
- System Info-ZIP displays Unicode names as question marks even for a Python
  reference archive, in all tested UTF-8 locales. Python and bsdtar read and
  extract Node's archive correctly, with matching names, CRCs and contents.
  Both the failing external-tool output and independent controls are retained.

The broad tests run on a recorded affinity set with one hardware thread from
16 different cores. This bounds `os.availableParallelism()`-scaled tests on
the 256-thread T7. The HTTP GC tests passed without changes under this bound;
the unbounded baseline created 1,024 concurrent requests and hit connection
resets. Previously timed-out coverage, flag-propagation, SQLite, ShadowRealm
and watch-mode tests also pass with appropriate timeouts. The final broad run
used eight workers and 120 seconds per case, taking about 651 seconds.

Directed validation:

- Ten snapshot/tiering configurations pass in both the main thread and workers,
  with heap verification and compacting GC. This includes forced native RegExp
  with the embedded snapshot, without the previous fallback flag.
- Inspector native conversion passes at 16 byte offsets for both overloads.
  The unchanged parent raises SIGBUS as the negative control. All 256 actual
  inspector Unicode round trips and property queries pass with heap checks.
- TypeScript 6.0.3 compiles a generated 64-module/65-file strict project, emits
  declarations and source maps, executes the emitted program (checksum
  2,207,808), reuses an incremental cache, rejects an intentional type error,
  recovers and runs the rebuilt output. ESLint 10.13.0 lints all 65 emitted
  files cleanly and catches an intentional undefined identifier. Compiler and
  linter logs prove automatic TurboFan compilation. Dependencies are pinned
  by `workloads/package-lock.json`.
- TypeScript 7's package entrypoint invokes a platform-specific native
  executable. The workload uses the 6.x JavaScript compiler to exercise Node's
  JavaScript execution and JIT. Native TypeScript packaging is a separate
  toolchain task.
- All 12 snapshot/cache state cases, 70,568 RegExp differential cases in three
  modes, 109/103/103 upstream RegExp cases, interrupt/GC/nested matching,
  optimized GC/deoptimization, serialization, addon, OSR and baseline
  regressions pass on the new binaries.
- npm workflows and Express HTTPS/ws/http-server checks pass. The 120.90-second
  application soak served 24,832 HTTPS requests with actual TurboFan execution
  in the handler and both workers. This is bounded validation, not a controlled
  performance comparison or long-term reliability claim.

