Scalar Wasm atomics milestone
----------------------------

Development checkouts: node-sparc64-wasm-atomics locally and
/root/node-sparc64-wasm-atomics on T7, branch sparc64-wasm-atomics. The verified
node-sparc64-wasm source tree and checkpoint are the preserved baseline.

The implementation adds naturally aligned atomic loads/stores and CASX loops
for scalar 8/16/32/64-bit RMW operations. RMW operations convert the selected
lane to Wasm little-endian values before arithmetic, then convert the desired
value back before CASX. A failed CAS retries from the current containing word,
preserving neighboring lanes. Full membar fences provide sequential consistency.
JavaScript atomics retain native-endian behavior through the existing helper's
default path. Wait/notify use V8's existing endian-aware futex runtime.
The Liftoff loop stack check now uses the interrupt-aware jslimit, following
PPC/s390, so a running worker services shared-memory growth requests. Both
unchanged upstream growth tests exposed the previous real_jslimit timeout;
the failed results and pre-fix source are retained as a negative control.

Validation passed on the current T7 build:

- test-wasm-atomics.js: 504 native Liftoff exports and 198,392 checks across
  memory32/64, shared/private memories, constant/dynamic addressing, all scalar
  widths and operations, LE byte layout, truncation, neighboring lanes and traps.
- prepare-wasm-atomics.py: pinned WebAssembly/threads atomic.wast, unchanged;
  WABT wast2json translates all 372 commands. run-wasm-atomic-spec.js executes
  every command, including 154 assert_return, 55 assert_trap and 93 invalid
  modules. The harness maps reference-interpreter diagnostics to V8 messages.
- Unchanged V8 atomic/CAS and shared-memory worker tests, invoked directly so
  that the upstream blanket big-endian Wasm skip cannot hide execution.
- build-wasm-threaded-workload.py: compile the bundled unchanged xxHash header
  with an atomic work queue; generate independent native C reference hashes.
  CLANG, WASM_LD and CC select the compilers. This is freestanding scalar Wasm;
  no Emscripten runtime, SIMD or optimizing Wasm compiler is required.
- test-wasm-threads.cjs: four Node workers hash 1,024 blocks (16 MiB) from a
  shared queue, compare every result to the native oracle, stress neighboring
  byte/halfword lanes and 32/64-bit counters, test 64-bit tearing, run 10,000
  store-buffering rounds, verify in-flight shared-memory growth, and check
  Wasm32/Wasm64 wait plus JavaScript/Wasm notification interoperability.

Reproduction after building and preparing the inputs:

    python3 tools/sparc64/test-wasm-atomics.py --group matrix
    python3 tools/sparc64/test-wasm-atomics.py --group spec
    python3 tools/sparc64/test-wasm-atomics.py --group upstream
    python3 tools/sparc64/test-wasm-atomics.py --group threads

The current native build passes all 198,392 matrix checks (504 Liftoff exports),
all 372 official atomic specification commands (73 native exports), and all 14
unchanged V8 atomic/shared-memory cases. Four Node workers hash 1,024 blocks
(16 MiB), with every result matching both the cross-host and native SPARC GCC
oracles. All workers perform work; 55 exports across the five instances are
verified as native Liftoff functions. Lane contention and 64-bit tearing run
10,003 iterations, store buffering runs 10,000 rounds, and all nine wait/notify
checks pass. A running worker observes shared-memory growth from 320 to 321
pages through the fixed interrupt-aware stack check.

Inherited regressions pass: 1,010 baseline Wasm checks, 73 scalar spec cases,
77 core JS API cases, the preserved 6,544-case ordinary JavaScript selection,
6,061 effective Node conformance cases including normal upstream skips, all
281 Node C++ tests, native RegExp, GC/deoptimization/OSR, snapshots/cache,
TypeScript/ESLint controls, npm and the two-minute HTTPS/WebSocket application
soak. Explicit JSPI and existing tool/platform exclusions remain unchanged;
permission, O_NOATIME and Unicode ZIP assumptions retain independent controls.

validate-wasm-atomics.py reproduces the acceptance phases. The checkpoint
profile wasm-atomics requires both audit-wasm.py and audit-wasm-atomics.py,
including current source/binary hashes, raw results, exact case selection,
the growth-test negative control, independent oracles and the unchanged
baseline source tree, binaries and checkpoint. Its path is recorded in
