JSPI stack switching (2026-10-29)
---------------------------------

The separate sparc64-jspi worktree starts from the verified scalar checkpoint
node-wasm-scalar-20261029T160442.922195Z (212 sources, 699 recorded artifacts).
The scalar parent remains unchanged. The current native Node, d8 and inspector
shell pass the JSPI acceptance described below. The full inherited regression
gates also pass: scalar Wasm, atomics, JavaScript JIT, Node conformance, GC/deopt,
RegExp, snapshots/cache, native addons, applications/npm and the two-minute
optimized application soak. Checkpoint closure requires the audits and separate
native/local verification described below.

Only two production files change relative to that parent. builtins-sparc64.cc
implements promise wrappers, suspension, resumption, rejection and CEntry
central-stack switching. setup-builtins-internal.cc adds SPARC64 to the guard
that emits the JSPI prompt exception-handler table. Generic JumpBuffer layout
and upstream test sources, assertions and expected output remain unchanged.

Native FP (%i6) is distinct from V8 FP (%l5); an extra unscanned JSPI frame slot
preserves it. Stack switches flush register windows and restore native and V8
frame pointers before changing SP. Fresh stacks need a mapped native window
anchor. These follow the register-window lessons in the Go SPARC runtime.
Logical SP translates through the existing stack-bias helpers (2047 native bias
plus 176-byte minimum native frame). CEntry checks exceptions on the central
stack; an unwinder retiring a secondary stack needs a safe central native anchor
and must restore native FP from the newly activated parent's saved JSPI frame.
ResolveWasmCodePointer also preserves its code-table index in g5 because loading
the external table base can clobber g1.

Native acceptance on T7:
- test-jspi-upstream.py: all five unchanged core JSPI suites and 13 related
  supported-mode regressions pass, with original per-file flags retained.
- test-jspi-node.py: unchanged parallel/test-wasm.mjs passes. Its former feature
  exclusion is removed from wasm-feature-exclusions.json in this child only.
- test-jspi-runtime.py: basic, full-compaction and incremental-marking profiles
  pass with heap verification. Each checks 128 repeated suspensions, eight
  concurrent stacks, eight nested promising calls, 16 rejections with recovery,
  64 stack reuses, Wasm catching rejected JS values, synchronous completion,
  parameter conversion and live externref/multi-value roots across GC. Each
  records 151 checks, 362 suspensions and 13 native Liftoff functions. The JS
  harness also passed an x86 Node 24 control with --experimental-wasm-jspi.
- test-jspi-termination.py: 20 repeated pairs of unchanged worker-termination
  cases pass (40 executions), including the full pool of 50 suspended stacks.
- build-jspi-inspector.py builds the unchanged upstream inspector shell against
  the existing V8 libraries without changing Node/d8. test-jspi-inspector.py
  passes debugger/wasm-jspi-async-stack with the original expected text, through
  the official runner. --run-skipped overrides its upstream architecture skip
  for this one test; the upstream status file remains unchanged.

The immutable scalar-parent binary traps in Node's test and 17 selected V8
cases; regress-408254017 already passes there. Negative controls and the three
implementation debugging attempts are retained under out/jspi-attempts.
prepare-jspi-inputs.py seeds pinned inputs and inherited negative controls from
the scalar checkpoint without overwriting current build logs or binaries.

Reproduction (native T7, cores disabled by the Python drivers):
  python3 tools/sparc64/test-jspi-upstream.py
  python3 tools/sparc64/test-jspi-node.py
  python3 tools/sparc64/test-jspi-runtime.py
  python3 tools/sparc64/test-jspi-termination.py
  python3 tools/sparc64/build-jspi-inspector.py
  python3 tools/sparc64/test-jspi-inspector.py
  python3 tools/sparc64/validate-wasm-atomics.py
  python3 tools/sparc64/test-wasm-scalar-acceptance.py
  python3 tools/sparc64/checkpoint.py --profile jspi

Preserve an existing inspector result before rerunning that driver. audit-jspi.py
requires current binary hashes, unchanged upstream fixture bytes, all original
scalar/atomics/Node/JIT/application gates, and a verified unchanged scalar parent.
The preserved Node conformance selection is 6,062 cases (5,832 broad, 137 addon,
93 native), with 478 normal upstream skips and no unexpected failures.
Inherited audits accept --allow-jspi only to account for the two production
changes and the newly enabled Node case. A JSPI checkpoint includes all inherited
evidence plus inspector binary/build logs, runtime profiles, termination rounds
and negative controls. verify-checkpoint.py checks every recorded artifact and
source-overlay member; a separate local mirror verifies the same native results.

Tests requiring optimizing Wasm (--no-liftoff), experimental growable stacks,
WasmFX, the unavailable Wasm interpreter or sandbox build remain separate
feature configurations. Ordinary non-promising exports still use the parent
architecture's compiled wrappers; the global generic-wrapper/stress-switch
architecture guard is unchanged. SIMD, optimizing Wasm, comprehensive Wasm GC proposal
execution and additional debugger/tiering support remain deferred. The passing
JSPI inspector test does not establish general Wasm debugger support. JavaScript
Sparkplug/TurboFan, native RegExp, snapshots/cache and scalar Liftoff remain
enabled. No commit or push has been performed.


Production Wasm (2026-10-30)
----------------------------

The sparc64-wasm-production worktree extends verified JSPI checkpoint
node-jspi-20261029T174932.476777Z. Its source tree, binaries and checkpoint
remain immutable. SIMD, Wasm GC, optimizing compilation, JS/Wasm wrappers and
supported body inlining, and general Wasm debugging/tiering are implemented.
The full inherited regression audit passes. The checkpoint command seals the
source overlay, native binaries and evidence; verify-checkpoint.py must pass on
both the native checkpoint and its local mirror before closing the goal.
Experimental WasmFX and growable stacks remain outside this milestone.

The 217 standard/relaxed SIMD emitters are shared by Liftoff and the optimizing
backend. A vector's high half occupies physical dN, its low half d(N+16), with
N=0..14 and d15/d31 reserved. Scalar dN therefore aliases the native high half.
Spills and ABI arguments use native big-endian 128-bit order; linear memory
uses Wasm little-endian order. Lazy compilation, debug breaks and deoptimization
preserve both halves. C ABI narrow integer arguments receive their required
sign or zero extension. Invalid floating-point arithmetic produces canonical
Wasm NaNs. Optional FP16 emitter hooks retain upstream fallback paths.

Normal upstream defaults are restored: Liftoff-only is false, Wasm inlining and
JS/Wasm call inlining are true. Feedback allocation and tiering budgets work;
optimized Wasm can deoptimize into Liftoff, including SIMD and float values.
Generic Wasm-to-JavaScript wrappers are enabled. Inspector breakpoint calls
have a fixed size across instruction alignment; debugger scope readers account
for native big-endian scalar slots and convert native vector lane order.

Native T7 feature evidence (label production-atomic-endian):
- All 258 pinned production specification scripts pass in Liftoff, optimizing
  and default modes, retaining three upstream expected failures. The 21 scripts
  for experimental WasmFX/custom descriptors are recorded separately.
- All 18 SIMD core cases and 22 GC core cases pass in both forced tiers;
  10 ABI cases also pass with normal defaults and lazy compilation.
- All 30 upstream deoptimization cases, 17 inlining/wrapper cases and four
  tiering cases pass with normal compiler defaults. Compiler traces prove
  Wasm-to-Wasm inlining and four supported Wasm bodies inlined into JavaScript.
- All 46 unchanged inspector Wasm debugger tests pass. An additional fixture
  inspects 20 distinct vectors across registers and spills before and after
  GC while paused, then verifies an order-sensitive resumed checksum.
- The BigInt-oracle SIMD matrix performs 11,328 calls and 1,812,480 byte/guard
  checks in each native tier. Undici's unchanged SIMD llhttp module performs
  1,320 parser checks per tier, with explicit native compiler proof.
- Three mixed SIMD/GC/JSPI profiles (normal, compaction, incremental marking)
  each check 34 calls and 102 promise suspensions across four concurrent stacks,
  retaining 20 vectors and Wasm GC objects before and after native tier-up.
- Sixteen of 17 additional GC/interoperability cases pass unchanged. The
  remaining instance-gc fixture writes through a host-endian Int32Array and
  expects Wasm's little-endian load to return the same value. Its unchanged
  failure is preserved. A separate copy changes only that write to explicit
  little-endian DataView.setInt32 and passes in all three compiler modes.
  This fixture limitation is reported explicitly by the audit; upstream files
  and test status files are unchanged. Upstream's status file otherwise skips
  all wasm/* mjsunit cases on big endian, which these targeted drivers bypass.

Reproduction and evidence:
  python3 tools/sparc64/prepare-wasm-production-regression-inputs.py
  python3 tools/sparc64/build-wasm.py --incremental
  python3 tools/sparc64/build-wasm-production-inspector.py --label LABEL
  python3 tools/sparc64/validate-wasm-production.py --stage prep --label LABEL
  python3 tools/sparc64/validate-wasm-production.py --stage features --label LABEL
  python3 tools/sparc64/validate-wasm-production.py --stage regressions --label LABEL
  python3 tools/sparc64/validate-wasm-production.py --stage optimizer-regressions --label LABEL
  python3 tools/sparc64/audit-wasm-production.py --label LABEL
  python3 tools/sparc64/checkpoint.py --profile wasm-production

Use a fresh label and preserve earlier attempts before rerunning legacy tests.
The feature stage retains the unchanged instance-gc failure and runs its control.
The final audit requires that exact failure, its byte-order probe and all three
corrected-copy controls, as well as every passing production and inherited gate.
The default checkpoint label is production-atomic-endian. Previous build attempts,
raw test output, negative controls and exact compiled source hashes are retained.
No commit, push or upstream publication has been performed.

Optimizing atomic RMW operations use little-endian semantics for Wasm linear
memory; JavaScript and tagged heap atomics retain native byte order, following
the s390 backend distinction. Both unchanged upstream compare-exchange stress
tests now pass. Optimized execution also passes 198,392 matrix checks across
504 native functions, 372 official atomic specification commands, and the real
16 MiB four-worker queue with lane contention, tearing, wait/notify, ordering
and memory growth. The previous timeouts and byte-swapped matrix result remain
preserved as negative controls. The extended fixtures explicitly verify the
native compilation tier and preserve separate baseline/optimizing evidence.

Final retained gates on the atomic-endian build: 6,062 Node cases (5,832 broad,
137 addon and 93 native cases, retaining upstream skips), 6,544 JavaScript
regressions, 281 C++ tests, all JSPI/scalar/atomic regressions, snapshot/cache
checks, three native RegExp profiles, npm/TypeScript/ESLint workflows and the
optimized two-minute application soak. The complete inherited audit chain and
the immutable Wasm, atomics, scalar and JSPI parents verify successfully.


Node feature completion work (2026-10-30)
---------------------------------------

The sparc64-node-features branch extends verified production checkpoint
node-wasm-production-20261029T234057.721541Z. The production parent is preserved.
Amaro, Temporal and FFI are enabled together in the validated Release build,
with Sparkplug, TurboFan, production Wasm, snapshots/cache and heap-verification
support. The combined feature and full inherited regression audits pass on T7.

Amaro passes all 39 relevant unchanged upstream Node tests without skips.
Its bundled Wasm parser passes 195 checks/99 calls in each native compiler tier,
including Unicode, generics, diagnostics and a 96 KB source file. A direct
TypeScript HTTP/fetch application with four TypeScript workers passes with cold
and warm compile caches; the immutable parent remains a negative control.

Temporal passes all three relevant Node cases and 13,056 pinned Test262
executions from 6,534 source files, retaining upstream expectations. Oslo DST
scheduling passes in the main isolate and four workers, normally and under
heap-verifying compaction. The original native Rust archive is unchanged.
A separate archive makes 56 overlapping weak C math symbols private, preventing
Rust's fallbacks from replacing platform libm and leaving signaling NaNs
unquieted. All 258 production Wasm specifications pass in each of the baseline,
optimizing and default modes with upstream expectations retained.

FFI supports native calls, callbacks and memory access, with an integer/pointer
fast-call trampoline for up to eight user arguments. Other signatures retain
libffi invocation. The standalone trampoline probe passes 135 ABI checks;
128 optimized native calls verify JIT return addresses, eight-argument ABI,
public-wrapper execution, GC and library closure, with FastApiCall graphs.
The separate shared-buffer body passes all 31 checks and the endian-corrected
seccomp fixture verifies executable-memory denial. Upstream FFI tests remain
unchanged: their BE skip guards and obsolete disabled-path arity expectation
are recorded explicitly, alongside separately derived passing controls.

Outgoing C argument slots are now included in V8's stack accounting so gap-move
temporaries cannot overwrite them. --v8-enable-verify-heap persists verification
support in config.gypi; a configure/regeneration control checks identical actual
compiler definitions, and the build helper rejects a missing VERIFY_HEAP.
Bundled libffi uses the SPARC PC-relative unwind encoding verified by the
native assembler/linker probe.

Retained validation includes 6,063 Node cases (5,833 broad, 137 addons and 93
native, with upstream skips retained), 6,544 JavaScript regressions, 281 C++
cases, all JSPI/scalar/atomic regressions, 46 Wasm inspector cases, optimization,
deoptimization/inlining, snapshot/cache, native RegExp, npm/TypeScript/ESLint
and the optimized two-minute application soak. The inherited instance-gc test
still assumes host little endian: its original failure is retained and a
separate DataView little-endian copy passes all three native compiler modes.

